# COMPANION SEARCH PATH for the snobol4 master suite -- corpus-relative dirs, IN ORDER, one per line.
# Read by SCRIP/scripts/lib_companion_closure.py at BUILD time only. The grader never reads this file: an entry is
# graded in an isolated temp dir and sees exactly the files copied in beside it, which is what makes a board reproducible.
#
# ⛔ THIS LISTS ONLY DIRS THE GRADER CANNOT REACH BY ITSELF. It does NOT list `include/`: the harness sets
# SNO_LIB=<corpus>/include on every m3 run and every m4 compile, so everything in that tree already resolves and copying
# it here would be churn. Declaring a reachable dir would turn 20 no-ops into 20 duplicated files.
#
# ⛔ AND IT IS A DECLARATION, NEVER A CORPUS-WIDE BASENAME SCAN, because a scan is a silent wrong answer:
# `VBGinTASA.dat` names three different files in this corpus and benchmarks/snocone/demo/VBGinTASA.dat is a DIFFERENT
# file (md5 48c78061) from the two identical snobol4 ones (98e80802). A basename index would have picked one by walk
# order and graded a snobol4 entry against another language's data with nothing in any output saying so.
packages/snobol4/gimpel    # OR.sno + the BALREV/REVERSE closure under it -- the gimpel probe witnesses' own sources,
                           # named by entries absorbed from that package and left behind by the absorption.
#
# ⛔ DELIBERATELY ABSENT -- demos/snobol4/claws5 and demos/snobol4/treebank. Their two .dat companions ARE unreachable,
# and materializing them makes things WORSE, not better: measured 2026-09-05, the refs of the two entries that name them
# (user_function_arbno_span_replace_branch_3, scrip_test_treebank-prepend) are the strings "Could not read
# CLAWS5inTASA.dat" and "Could not read VBGinTASA.dat" -- each entry is pinned GREEN to its own missing-dependency error
# message. Supplying the data turns both RED (and treebank-prepend then emits 133 MB, which is no kind of suite entry).
# That is a faulty-test question for whoever owns the ref, not a search-path one; recorded in
